::ENG4_U03_L05_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage A: The town should install public water refill stations because bottled-water cleanup costs increased by 11 percent. Passage B: The town should install refill stations because residents should not have to buy plastic bottles to access water at public parks. Which comparison is strongest?
{
=A. Passage A uses cost evidence, while Passage B uses fairness and access to frame the same issue.#Correct
~B. Passage A and Passage B use the same type of evidence.#Incorrect
~C. Passage B is irrelevant because it does not mention cleanup costs.#Incorrect
~D. Passage A has no claim because it includes a statistic.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: An article argues that flexible deadlines improve student work. It cites lower late-submission rates, stronger revision scores, and fewer missing assignments. How effective is the support?
{
~A. Ineffective, because all evidence must come from personal testimony.#Incorrect
=C. Effective, because the evidence connects flexibility to completion and quality.#Correct
~B. Ineffective, because revision scores are unrelated to student work.#Incorrect
~D. Effective only because it contains three details.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.07] Passage: A letter to parents argues for a senior internship day by emphasizing workplace readiness, scheduling details, and student supervision. What purpose most likely shapes this approach?
{
~A. To entertain parents with a story about internships.#Incorrect
~C. To make parents anxious without giving details.#Incorrect
=B. To persuade parents that the event is useful and responsibly planned.#Correct
~D. To avoid explaining why the internship day matters.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage A calls a change a "limited trial." Passage B calls the same change an "urgent correction." What does this wording difference reveal?
{
~A. Passage A is more emotional than Passage B.#Incorrect
~B. Passage B refuses to take a position.#Incorrect
~C. Both phrases have identical tone.#Incorrect
=D. Passage A frames the change cautiously, while Passage B frames it as necessary and immediate.#Correct
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage A supports adding bike lanes with crash data. Passage B supports adding bike lanes with a commuter's personal experience. Which evaluation best fits a transportation committee?
{
=A. Passage A is likely stronger because crash data connects directly to planning and safety decisions.#Correct
~B. Passage B is automatically stronger because personal stories are more memorable.#Incorrect
~C. Both arguments are ineffective because they use different evidence.#Incorrect
~D. Passage A is weaker because it does not describe feelings.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage A: "The tutoring center should open earlier because 47 students requested help before first period." Passage B: "The tutoring center should open earlier because students should not have to choose between help and their after-school jobs." Which answer best compares the reasoning?
{
=C. Passage A uses a documented need, while Passage B reasons from student access and fairness.#Correct
~B. Passage A has no reasoning because it includes a number.#Incorrect
~A. Passage B has no reasoning because it does not include a number.#Incorrect
~D. Both passages use identical reasoning.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: A report claims that peer mentoring improves school culture. It supports this with fewer discipline referrals, increased club participation, and student survey comments about belonging. Which judgment is best?
{
~A. The support is weak because culture cannot be supported with evidence.#Incorrect
=B. The support is strong because it connects behavior, participation, and student perception to school culture.#Correct
~C. The support is weak because survey comments are always invalid.#Incorrect
~D. The support is strong only because it includes discipline data.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.R.07] Passage: An editorial opens by describing crowded sidewalks near school, then argues for staggered dismissal times. What author purpose does the opening serve?
{
~A. It proves that all students dislike dismissal.#Incorrect
~B. It distracts readers from the proposed solution.#Incorrect
=D. It frames the proposal as a response to a visible safety and crowding problem.#Correct
~C. It shows the author has no clear claim.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: One writer calls a budget cut a "temporary reduction," while another calls it a "harmful setback." Which answer best explains the rhetorical effect?
{
=A. The first phrase softens the change, while the second phrase emphasizes damage.#Correct
~B. Both phrases make the cut sound equally positive.#Incorrect
~C. The second phrase is neutral and technical.#Incorrect
~D. The first phrase creates urgency.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage A argues for a four-day cleanup event using volunteer sign-up data. Passage B argues for it by describing the riverbank as "a public space we have neglected." Which comparison is most accurate?
{
=C. Passage A appeals to feasibility, while Passage B appeals to shared responsibility.#Correct
~B. Passage B is unrelated because it does not mention volunteers.#Incorrect
~A. Passage A is not an argument because it uses data.#Incorrect
~D. Both passages use only emotional appeal.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: A commentary argues that remote work can improve productivity. It cites reduced commute time, longer uninterrupted work blocks, and employee reports of fewer distractions. Which critique is most reasonable?
{
=B. The support is relevant, but it would be stronger if it also addressed jobs that require in-person work.#Correct
~A. The support is useless because productivity cannot be measured.#Incorrect
~C. The support is unrelated to the claim.#Incorrect
~D. The support proves remote work is best for every worker.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.R.07] Passage: A speech to graduating seniors uses direct address, repeated phrases, and examples of difficult choices after high school. What purpose do these choices most likely serve?
{
~A. To make the speech sound detached and technical.#Incorrect
=D. To build a personal, motivating connection with the audience.#Correct
~C. To hide the speaker's message.#Incorrect
~B. To present a budget proposal.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage A says online forms reduce processing time by 20 percent. Passage B says online forms respect residents' time by removing unnecessary office visits. Which evaluation is best?
{
=A. Passage A emphasizes measurable efficiency, while Passage B translates efficiency into a resident-centered value.#Correct
~B. Passage B contradicts Passage A.#Incorrect
~C. Passage A is emotional because it uses a percentage.#Incorrect
~D. Passage B has no claim.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: A writer describes a proposal as "modest, targeted, and reversible." What effect does this diction create?
{
~A. It makes the proposal seem reckless.#Incorrect
=C. It makes the proposal seem practical and low-risk.#Correct
~B. It makes the proposal seem unrelated to policy.#Incorrect
~D. It makes the proposal seem humorous.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage A argues that a school should add outdoor seating because cafeteria capacity is limited. Passage B argues that outdoor seating would make lunch feel less crowded and more humane. Which audience would likely find Passage A more effective?
{
=B. A facilities committee making space and capacity decisions.#Correct
~A. A student poetry club discussing imagery.#Incorrect
~C. A group looking only for personal stories.#Incorrect
~D. Readers who oppose all school changes.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: A proposal claims that adding shaded seating will improve park use. It cites heat index data, survey responses from older residents, and observations that benches are empty during midday. Which answer best evaluates the support?
{
=D. It is effective because the evidence connects weather, user needs, and current behavior to the claim.#Correct
~B. It is ineffective because parks cannot be studied.#Incorrect
~C. It is ineffective because all evidence must be a statistic.#Incorrect
~A. It is effective only because older residents are mentioned.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.R.07] Passage: An article about emergency alerts begins with a timeline of recent storm warnings before arguing for improved text-message notifications. What purpose does the timeline serve?
{
=A. It shows a pattern that makes the proposed notification change seem necessary.#Correct
~B. It replaces the article's claim.#Incorrect
~C. It makes the problem seem imaginary.#Incorrect
~D. It avoids connecting evidence to purpose.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage A uses a chart of rising rental costs to argue for housing assistance. Passage B uses a story about a family moving twice in one year. Which evaluation is best?
{
=C. Passage A builds broad economic context, while Passage B humanizes the impact of that context.#Correct
~B. Passage B is invalid because stories cannot support arguments.#Incorrect
~A. Passage A is invalid because charts are not words.#Incorrect
~D. Both passages use the same rhetorical method.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: One text calls a delay "administrative caution." Another calls it "avoidable inaction." What is the main rhetorical difference?
{
=B. The first phrase justifies delay, while the second criticizes it.#Correct
~A. Both phrases praise the delay.#Incorrect
~C. Neither phrase shows attitude.#Incorrect
~D. The second phrase is more neutral.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage A argues for school gardens using science curriculum connections. Passage B argues for school gardens using student wellness benefits. Which synthesis best compares the claims?
{
=D. Both support school gardens, but Passage A emphasizes academic value and Passage B emphasizes personal well-being.#Correct
~B. Passage A opposes gardens because it mentions curriculum.#Incorrect
~C. Passage B is unrelated because wellness is not education.#Incorrect
~A. Both passages make no claim.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: A news analysis claims that local theaters strengthen downtown economies. It cites restaurant sales on show nights, parking revenue, and interviews with nearby shop owners. What is the best evaluation?
{
=A. The support is relevant because it connects theater attendance to surrounding business activity.#Correct
~B. The support is weak because interviews can never be useful.#Incorrect
~C. The support proves theaters are the only cause of downtown growth.#Incorrect
~D. The support is unrelated because theaters are cultural, not economic.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.R.07] Passage: A public health announcement uses simple sentences, direct commands, and repeated reminders. What is the likely purpose of this style?
{
=C. To make directions clear and memorable for a broad audience.#Correct
~B. To make the message harder to follow.#Incorrect
~A. To avoid influencing the audience.#Incorrect
~D. To imitate academic research writing.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage A argues that a tutoring app saves time by matching students quickly. Passage B argues that the app gives quiet students a less intimidating way to ask for help. Which answer best evaluates the different emphases?
{
=B. Passage A emphasizes efficiency, while Passage B emphasizes access and student comfort.#Correct
~A. Passage B is unrelated because it does not discuss time.#Incorrect
~C. Passage A is emotional because it mentions time.#Incorrect
~D. Both passages oppose the tutoring app.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: A policy is described as "balanced" in one text and "watered-down" in another. What does the diction suggest?
{
~A. Both authors view the policy positively.#Incorrect
=D. The first author frames compromise as reasonable, while the second frames it as weakness.#Correct
~C. The second author has no opinion.#Incorrect
~B. The first author uses hostile language.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage A supports a college application workshop with data on missed deadlines. Passage B supports it with the statement, "Talent should not be limited by paperwork confusion." Which answer best evaluates the stronger argument for counselors?
{
=A. Passage A may be stronger because counselors need evidence of a recurring procedural problem.#Correct
~B. Passage B is stronger because it sounds more inspiring.#Incorrect
~C. Passage A is weaker because it does not mention talent.#Incorrect
~D. Both arguments are ineffective because they support the same workshop.#Incorrect
}
